\begin_layout Title
Analog Lab: Impedance, Filters, Amplifiers, and Noise
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
By Patrick Kantorski
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
February 8, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This report was written for a radio astronomy undergraduate course at UC
 Berkeley.
 Through combining a FM demodulator with an amplifier and speaker, FM radio
 signals can be converted into sound.
 Circuits constructed in this lab were built around demodulating a 1.045
 MHz FM radio transmission.
 Coding was also done in the programming language Python to examine Johnson-Nyqu
ist noise and demonstrate the Central Limit Theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
I
\begin_inset space \enskip{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 1.55em
\end_inset

An analog signal is any continuous signal in which the time variance is
 characterized by some other quantity that varies with time.
 In this lab, circuits made from basic circuitry components were constructed,
 analyzed, and implemented on analog signals.
 By using resistors, capacitors, and inductors, my partners and I assembled
 voltage dividers, high-pass filters, low-pass filters, and even bandpass
 filters in order to produce a functioning FM radio receiver.
 Additionally, concepts such as signal reflection, signal transmission,
 termination, impedance matching of transmission lines, Johnson-Nyquist
 noise, and the Central Limit Theorem were also examined as the lab progressed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Signal reflection occurs when the impedance of a transmission line is mismatched.
 What signal reflection literally is is the reflection of a signal that
 is being passed through a transmission line.
 When reflection occurs, the transmission of the signal is reduced and the
 reflected signal reverts back up the transmission line.
 To fix this problem, all that needs to be done is to properly terminate
 the transmission line.
 Proper termination is done by matching the impedance between connections.
 Additionally, to minimize loading and achieve more accurate measurements,
 scope probes with attenuation factors are used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Johnson-Nyquist noise is the thermal noise produced by the random collisions
 of electrons within a resistor.
 The random thermal motion of the electrons produces a current that has
 zero mean but a non-zero variance.
 The current also sets up a small fluctuating voltage across the resistor.
 Many random processes or factors, including the random motion of the electrons
 in Johnson-Nyquist noise, converge to Gaussian distributions.
 When adding together random numbers, the variance of the sum is the sum
 of the variances of the numbers.
 These two concepts are at the heart of the Central Limit Theorem and were
 also examined in this lab.
\end_layout

