FM Radio Receiver
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
An FM radio receiver, or FM demodulator, functions by converting FM radio
 signals into modulated carrier wave signals.
 The term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demodulation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 refers to the process of extracting a signal carrying information from
 a modulated carrier wave.
 The demodulator takes the digital data creates the analog signal.
 The created analog signal also passes through a low-pass filter for smoothing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
B
\begin_inset space \enskip{}
\end_inset

Amplifier for FM Signal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to increase the volume of the FM signal after being demodulated,
 an amplifier was constructed based around an NPN transistor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To test the amplifier and check if it functioned with the appropriate gain,
 the function generator and oscilloscope were used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Modifications were made after construction to increase the amplifier's gain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
C
\begin_inset space \enskip{}
\end_inset

Johnson-Nyquist Noise and The Central Limit Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As mentioned in the introduction, Johnson-Nyquist noise is the thermal noise
 produced by the random motion of electrons within a resistor.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The noise factor is defined as the ratio of the output noise power of a
 device to the portion of the power attributed to thermal noise at the input
 at the standard noise temperature.
 The noise factor could also be thought of as the ratio of actual output
 noise to that which would remain if the device itself did not introduce
 noise.
\end_layout

