\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
RLC Bandpass Filter
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
The first three components on Figure 1 work together as a RLC bandpass filter.
 Bandpass filters work by passing frequencies within a certain range and
 attenuating frequencies outside that range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
To isolate the 1.045 MHz FM signal, the bandpass (see Figure 2) was constructed
 based on the following equation.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Equations: (1) 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{0}=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 2em
\end_inset

(2) 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=2\pi f_{0}RC=\frac{f_{0}}{\triangle f_{3dB}}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 2em
\end_inset

(3) 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{0}=2\pi f_{0}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset space \hspace{}
\length 2em
\end_inset

(4) 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}=\left(\frac{j\omega L}{1-\omega^{2}LC}\right)/\left(R+\frac{j\omega L}{1-\omega^{2}LC}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $f_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the bandpass's center frequency, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the angular frequency representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{0}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 adjusts the filter's roll-off.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Using the values shown on Figure 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{0}\thickapprox1.073\times10^{6}Hz$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\thickapprox4.895$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{0}\approx6.741\times10^{6}Hz$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

