\begin_layout Subsection*
B
\begin_inset space \enskip{}
\end_inset

Johnson-Nyquist Noise and The Central Limit Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
After learning and experimenting with voltage amplifiers in the first part
 of the lab, examining Johnson-Nyquist noise made a lot more sense.
 Because the produced thermal noise in a resistor is fairly small, amplifying
 the noise signal can lead to further statistical analysis on the properties
 of the noise.
 Additionally, the noise figure of a circuit can be viewed as the measure
 of the circuit's signal noise.
 The noise figure also defines the performance, specifications, and properties
 of the circuit as a radio receiver.
 In radio astronomy, the flux of different stars observed by a telescope
 can be contributed to the thermal noise produced in the receiver itself.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
By implementing and demonstrating the Central Limit Theorem in Python, it
 became more obvious as to how non-Gaussian random distributions converge
 to Gaussian distributions.
 Because ensembles of many different random processes converge to Gaussian
 distributions, normal distributions are everywhere; including in Johnson-Nyquis
t noise.
 Also, when adding together random numbers or distributions of numbers,
 the variance of the sum is equivalent to the sum of the variance of the
 numbers.
 This means that if a group of samples are drawn from the same distribution
 and averaged, then the standard deviation will decrease as the square root
 of the number of samples averaged.
 This is why a better estimate can be achieved by making a bunch of independent
 measurements taken as the square-root of the number of measurements.
\end_layout

